I’ll inspect the measurement metadata and diffraction pattern first, then use the available search/indexing and GSAS-II tools to identify candidate phases, refine them jointly, and validate that the exported CIFs, profile, fractions, and project are internally consistent.

The chemistry strongly suggests a magnesium tungstate system, but I won’t assume a single product: I’m checking peak positions, residual precursor signatures, and whether any secondary magnesium–tungsten oxide is required before building the refinement.

Initial peak matching supports three chemically plausible crystalline components: wolframite-type MgWO₄ (including the very strong 18.88° line), monoclinic/triclinic WO₃ (the 23.1–24.4° multiplet), and residual rocksalt MgO (42.93°, 62.26°, and higher-angle lines). I’m now testing structural variants and quantitative necessity rather than treating those assignments as settled.

The candidate structures reproduce the diagnostic line positions closely, including the small MgO contribution. I’m starting with a restrained three-phase refinement (background, relative phase scales, cells/zero, then peak shape) so phase fractions are not allowed to compensate for incorrect peak positions or widths.

The three-phase model is compositionally complete—unmodeled residuals do not form an extra coherent peak set—but MgWO₄ intensities show pronounced texture: its (100) reflection at 18.88° is much stronger, while the 15.56° line is weaker, than a random powder predicts. I’m testing a single physically interpretable March–Dollase orientation term and phase-specific broadening before changing atomic coordinates.

A stable, physically constrained profile model now reaches \(R_{wp}\approx2.62\%\) without an extra phase. It requires much broader WO₃ peaks (about 0.22 µm coherent-size term and ~0.7% microstrain), while MgO’s size term is not identifiable from its few weak lines; I’m fixing that unconstrained term and testing only structurally meaningful coordinate/displacement refinements.

The final model is now statistically near the counting-noise limit (\(R_{wp}\approx1.65\%\), GOF ≈ 1.08). The MgO assignment is quantitatively necessary: omitting it raises \(R_{wp}\) to 2.51%, while omitting WO₃ raises it to 17.1%. I’m producing the final project and exports from one last converged refinement, then I’ll validate every artifact against that project.

